From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikolajs Agafonovs <nikolajs.agafonovs@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 19:02:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] tests: 168_if0_label and 169_cleanup_assign, the cases of
 fixes 126 and 127; 161 and 164 made deterministic in the musl guests

168: a constant if whose dead branch holds a label reached by a goto or
an asm goto (forward, with else, the else dead, nested, a return under
the label, and the kernel's get_user_inline() shape as a statement
expression with a local label).  169: the destination of an assignment
whose right-hand side is a statement expression with a cleanup variable
(plain, compound, a struct member, a global, chained, a for loop with the
cleanup variable, and the return, condition, operand and argument uses
that were fine before).  Expected output from gcc -O0, the same at -O2.

Two existing tests failed only inside the self-hosting guests, the first
run of the suite there since 2026-10-23: 164_inline_call printed two
label addresses with %p, one of them null (glibc writes "(nil)", musl
"0"); printed as integers.  161_gnu_extensions2 checked (long)&e % 16 on
an aligned(16) local; the i386 backend does not realign the stack, so
that depends on the caller's frame and differed between the first- and
second-generation compilers' -run harnesses (the compiled test was
byte-identical); the aligned variable is a static one now, whose
address the data section's alignment makes deterministic.  Both
expectations are unchanged apart from 164's line.
